<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uqair Protocol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Uqair Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair">Uqair</a> Protocol of 1922 set the borders between the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> and the British-protected Kuwait and mandated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Imposed at a conference at the port of Uqair by the British high commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-cox">Sir Percy Cox</a>, it gave Nejd about two-thirds of the land claimed by Kuwait. It also created two neutral zones, shared by Nejd with Kuwait and with Iraq.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uqair-protocol.jpg" alt="Uqair Protocol" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States. Central Intelligence Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_East._LOC_95681206.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
